Permission modes


Manual mode
Reads only, without prompting. Everything else asks first.

AcceptEdits on
Runs reads, file edits, and common file system bash commands without asking. This is for iterating on code that you review after the fact.

Plan mode
Researches and proposes changes without editing anything.
Reads run freely, and when auto mode is available, auto's classifier reviews the commands Claude runs to explore and lets the approved ones run without a prompt.

Auto 
Accepts everything, with a separate classifier model reviewing most actions before they run.
Auto is the hands-off mode. Claude runs on its own, but before most actions execute,
a separate classifier model reviews them. The classifier guards intent. It's watching for moves that escalate beyond what you actually asked for.

Here's the kind of thing it's designed to block:
Production deploys and migrations
Force pushing, or piping downloaded code straight into a shell
Sending sensitive data to external endpoints
Irreversibly destroying files that existed before the session
claude auto-mode defaults prints the full list.

And it waves through the everyday work: local edits in your project, installing dependencies from your lock file,
read-only requests, and pushing to any branch of the repository you're working in, including the default branch. 
The exception is any other branch whose name marks it as a deploy target, such as production or gh-pages. The classifier judges a push there on its own terms.

The classifier checks intent, not correctness. It won't catch whether the code actually works.
So if you ask Claude to refactor authentication and it writes broken authentication, the classifier waves it through, because broken isn't dangerous.

That's why you pair auto mode with a stop hook that runs your tests. The two work together:

Auto mode watches what Claude is trying to do while it runs.
The stop hook confirms the code actually runs once Claude finishes.

One guards intent before most actions, the other guards correctness after. Auto mode's guardrails are still evolving, so check the docs for the current block and allow lists.



Don't ask
Allows file reads inside your working directories and pre-approved tools. Anything that would prompt is auto-denied.

Claude still runs what needs no approval in Manual mode, such as file reads inside your working directories, plus the tools you pre-approved. 

Anything that would otherwise prompt gets auto-denied. 

Claude auto-denies anything that would prompt. Allow rules and PreToolUse hook approvals still run. 

Explicit ask rules, AskUserQuestion, and critical-path removals are denied rather than prompted. Cloud sessions ignore it when it comes from settings files.
CI pipelines, scheduled jobs, overnight batches. That's the whole point. Your pipeline keeps moving instead of hanging on an approval no one is there to give.
You set it with --permission-mode dontAsk

Bypass Permission
Skips permission prompts, apart from a few actions that no mode approves on its own.
This is the equivalent of the dangerously-skip-permissions flag. Only run it inside an isolated container or virtual machine.
